+# The Daily Grind
+
+Static homepage. The equipment panel runs entirely in the visitor's browser; it
+does not proxy private addresses through the website's server.
+
+## Equipment integration
+
+- Serve the production homepage over HTTPS. Localhost is a secure-context
+  exception for development.
+- The editable example endpoints are `http://192.168.1.100/api/status` for a LAN
+  brewer and `http://127.0.0.1:45678/health` for a desktop helper. Configure them
+  to match the actual firmware/app. No vendor API or helper installation is
+  bundled with this static page.
+- Each endpoint must support a read-only GET with a readable HTTP 2xx response.
+  No particular body format is required. Failure status codes are displayed.
+  Redirects are rejected; use the final endpoint URL.
+- Configure the device/helper to return `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` with the
+  exact homepage origin. Requests omit credentials, cookies, and referrer; no
+  authentication headers are sent. Do not use a state-changing endpoint.
+- LAN requests declare `targetAddressSpace: 'local'` and query `local-network`;
+  companion requests declare `targetAddressSpace: 'loopback'` and query
+  `loopback-network`. Permission queries are caught on unsupported browsers;
+  connection attempts remain explicit user actions. No deprecated PNA headers
+  or legacy combined permission queries are used.
+- Browsers without support may block local HTTP even when the endpoint permits
+  CORS. Use a supporting browser or an HTTPS endpoint with a trusted certificate.
+  Permission cannot be granted by the website itself.
+- Status checks are independent, cancellable, and time out after 20 seconds.
+  Success reports the check time, not continuous device monitoring. Disconnect
+  only clears page state. Addresses are not persisted or sent to a server.
+
+Platform references: [Local network access](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/Security/Defenses/Local_network_access),
+[CORS](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Guides/CORS).
